# 232: one more step of 231's cycle crosses the limit. `a(500)` produces
# 1 + 2*500 = 1001 invocations; invocation #1000 is `b(0)` running at depth
# 1000, and the call site refused is the one INSIDE `b` — line 6 — not the one
# inside `a` at line 3. So this case pins the reported line to whichever call
# site actually happened to be the 1001st, which in a mutual cycle is not the
# function the program called.
# The message is `error::recursion_limit`, identical to 226's: one constructor,
# one `MAX_DEPTH`, so no engine and no call site can respell it.
--- source
fn a(n) {
  if n == 0 { return 0; }
  return b(n - 1);
}
fn b(n) {
  return a(n);
}
print a(500);
--- expect
error: Value at line 6: recursion limit of 1000 active invocations exceeded
